{"id":"1f8b1ce2-c403-477b-ad65-5b82374281b5","object":"chat.completion","created":1784977728,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    round_scores = case['round_scores']\\n    round_targets = case['round_targets']\\n    forced = case['forced']\\n    N = len(round_scores[0])\\n    active = set(range(N))\\n    forced_set = set(forced)\\n    history = []\\n    for r in range(len(round_scores)):\\n        scores = round_scores[r]\\n        target = round_targets[r]\\n        forced_in_active = forced_set & active\\n        num_forced = len(forced_in_active)\\n        if num_forced > target:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Infeasible target\\\")\\n        non_forced_active = active - forced_set\\n        sorted_candidates = sorted(\\n            [(scores[i], i) for i in non_forced_active],\\n            key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1])\\n        )\\n        selected_non_forced = [idx for _, idx in sorted_candidates[:target - num_forced]]\\n        selected = set(forced_in_active) | set(selected_non_forced)\\n        selected_sorted = sorted(selected)\\n        history.append(selected_sorted)\\n        active = selected\\n    retained_indices = sorted(active)\\n    return {'history': history, 'retained_indices': retained_indices}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that implements iterative token compression. The case payload contains:\n\n- `round_scores`: list of lists of floats, each inner list is scores for tokens in that round.\n- `round_targets`: list of ints, the number of tokens to keep in each round.\n- `forced`: list of ints, indices that must be kept in all rounds (if present in that round? Actually forced indices are global? The description says \"preserving forced tokens and original order\". In fixtures, forced is a list of indices. Probably forced indices must be kept in all rounds if they are still active. They are indices into the original token sequence.)\n\nWe need to perform iterative compression: start with all token indices (0 to N-1 where N is length of first round scores? But round_scores might have multiple rounds, each round is a list of scores for the same set of tokens? Actually in fixtures, there are 2 rounds, each with 8 scores. So there are 8 tokens. In each round, we have a new set of scores (maybe from different model) for those tokens. The goal: select a set of token indices to keep across all rounds, such that in each round, among the currently active indices, we keep exactly `round_targets[round]` indices, but we must also include forced indices (if they are still active). The order must be preserved (i.e., the retained indices should be in increasing order of original indices).\n\nThe description: \"Begin with all original token indices active. At each round, rank only currently active indices by descending supplied score. Insert forced indices before filling remaining positions. Preserve original order in every retained mask and reject infeasible targets. Return the per-round masks and the final retained indices.\"\n\nSo algorithm:\n\nLet N = number of tokens (length of round_scores[0]).\nactive = set of all indices 0..N-1.\nhistory = [] list of lists (each round's mask, list of indices kept in that round)\nretained_indices = [] final list\n\nFor each round r (0 to len(round_scores)-1):\n  scores = round_scores[r] list of floats for all tokens.\n  target = round_targets[r]\n  forced_set = set(forced) (but forced indices that are not in active? They should be in active initially, but after rounds, some may have been dropped. However forced tokens must be preserved, so they should never be dropped. So we assume forced indices are always kept. But we need to handle infeasibility: if target is less than number of forced indices in active, then it's infeasible. Also if target > len(active) then infeasible. Probably we can ignore infeasible cases as they won't appear in test? But contract says \"reject infeasible targets\" – we can raise ValueError or something? But the function must return a JSON-serializable object without errors. Probably the input will be feasible.\n\nIn each round:\n- Among active indices, we have forced indices (must keep) and others.\n- We need to select exactly target indices to keep for this round, including forced indices. So we first include all forced indices that are in active.\n- Then we need to fill remaining slots (target - len(forced_already_active)) with the highest-scoring among the non-forced active indices, based on their scores in this round.\n- Then we set active = set(selected indices for this round). But careful: \"Insert forced indices before filling remaining positions.\" Means forced indices take priority.\n- The order in the mask: \"Preserve original order in every retained mask\". So the list of indices kept in this round should be sorted in increasing order (original index order). So after selection, we sort the selected indices.\n\n- history.append(sorted list of kept indices for this round)\n\nAfter all rounds, retained_indices = sorted list of active indices (which should be the same as the last round's selected indices). So retained_indices = sorted(list(active)). Actually after last round, active is the set of indices kept. So retained_indices = sorted(active). But the description says \"final retained indices\", which are the indices kept after all rounds. That should be the last round's kept indices, same as active.\n\nWe should note that forced indices may be outside the current active set if they were dropped in previous round? But forced indices should never be dropped. So we must ensure that forced indices are always selected. In our algorithm, at each round we first include all forced indices that are in active. But if a forced index was not in active (impossible if we never dropped it), then it's an error. So we assume forced indices are always active.\n\nAlso, \"round_scores\" might have multiple rounds, each with same length N. But round_scores[0] length determines N.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) that reads these fields and returns object with \"history\" (list of lists of ints) and \"retained_indices\" (list of ints).\n\nEdge cases: forced indices may be empty. Also ensure we handle duplicates? Not needed.\n\nNow write Python code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    round_scores = case['round_scores']\n    round_targets = case['round_targets']\n    forced = case['forced']\n    N = len(round_scores[0])\n    active = set(range(N))\n    forced_set = set(forced)\n    history = []\n    for r in range(len(round_scores)):\n        scores = round_scores[r]\n        target = round_targets[r]\n        # active indices\n        # forced indices that are in active (should be all)\n        forced_in_active = forced_set & active\n        num_forced = len(forced_in_active)\n        if num_forced > target:\n            # infeasible, but we'll just keep forced? or raise? We'll raise ValueError to indicate infeasibility? But spec says \"reject infeasible targets\" - maybe we should raise, but that would break JSON output. Safer to handle by keeping only forced? But target must be exactly that many. I think test cases will be feasible.\n            # We'll proceed but this might cause wrong output. Better to raise, but we must not raise exceptions. Let's assume feasible.\n            pass\n        # candidates for selection from non-forced active\n        non_forced_active = active - forced_set\n        # sort by descending score, then by index? Actually we need highest scores, but scores might have ties? Not likely. We'll sort by (-score, index) to break ties arbitrarily but we need stable. Actually we just want highest scores; if tie, any order works but preserve original order? The selection is for indices to keep, and then we sort the whole kept set by original index. So tie-breaking doesn't matter for final mask because we sort anyway. So we can just select the top (target - num_forced) indices from non_forced_active based on their scores.\n        # Build list of (score, index) for non_forced_active, sort descending by score, then take first k.\n        sorted_candidates = sorted([(scores[i], i) for i in non_forced_active], key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\n        selected_non_forced = [idx for _, idx in sorted_candidates[:target - num_forced]]\n        # combine\n        selected = set(forced_in_active) | set(selected_non_forced)\n        # sort by index to preserve original order\n        selected_sorted = sorted(selected)\n        history.append(selected_sorted)\n        active = selected\n    retained_indices = sorted(active)\n    return {\"history\": history, \"retained_indices\": retained_indices}\n\nCheck with first fixture: round_scores[0] list, round_targets [6,4], forced [5]. Let's simulate quickly.\n\nN=8, active all 0..7.\nRound0: forced_in_active={5}. target=6, num_forced=1. non_forced_active = {0,1,2,3,4,6,7}. Scores for round0: [0.44799538,0.88373271,0.48037242,0.3104939,0.99600231,0.51643342,0.68752286,0.33943666] indices 0..7. Sort non_forced by score desc: index4 (0.996), index1(0.8837), index6(0.6875), index2(0.48037), index0(0.44799), index7(0.33944), index3(0.31049). Need 5 more (target-1=5). So pick indices [4,1,6,2,0]. Combine with forced 5 => {0,1,2,4,5,6}. Sorted: [0,1,2,4,5,6]. So history[0] = [0,1,2,4,5,6].\n\nRound1: active = {0,1,2,4,5,6}. round_targets[1]=4. forced_in_active = {5} (assuming forced still active). non_forced_active = {0,1,2,4,6}. Scores for round1: [0.07094129,0.34602012,0.53826085,0.26734445,0.39865641,0.60305915,0.70890049,0.77082382] indices. Sort non_forced by score desc: index6 (0.7089), index2(0.53826), index4(0.39866), index1(0.34602), index0(0.07094). Need (4-1)=3 more, pick [6,2,4]. Combine with forced 5 => {2,4,5,6}. Sorted: [2,4,5,6]. So history[1] = [2,4,5,6]. retained_indices = [2,4,5,6].\n\nNow check if forced index 5 was preserved. Yes.\n\nSecond fixture: for